      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3350.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 759,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of hydrogen and oxygen. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is arguably the most important substance for life on Earth. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Three States)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we normally drink and what fills our oceans, rivers, and lakes.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and steam.\n\n### 2. What Makes Water Unique?\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport medium, carrying vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients in living things and in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and get denser when they freeze. Water, however, **expands** and becomes *less dense* when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a glass. In nature, this means lakes freeze from the top down, allowing fish and aquatic life to survive the winter underneath the ice.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees to the leaves).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite this abundance, about **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans. Only 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and shallow aquifers).\n\n### 4. Why is Water Important for Life?\n*   **Biology:** Your body is made of about 60% water. Brains are roughly 73% water, and lungs are about 83%. Water regulates human body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and enables cellular processes. Without water, humans can only survive for about 3 to 5 days.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Water is the habitat for millions of aquatic species and forms the foundation of all global ecosystems. \n*   **Civilization:** Human history revolves around water. Every major ancient civilization (like ancient Egypt along the Nile, or Mesopotamia along the Tigris and Euphrates) was built near a water source for drinking, agriculture, and trade.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, shaping our geography, driving our weather, and making the existence of biological life possible."
